    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc" resp="#VM">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1" style="text-direction:l-to-r"/><gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character"/>ΤΑΙ<unclear>Ν</unclear><gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character"/>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <listApp>
                    <app><note>Text: Bertaiola et al. 2023, pp. 383-388.</note></app>
                    <app><note>The fourth letter is read tentatively as nu by the editors.</note></app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#VM">
                <p cert="low">[Dedicated] to the two goddesses.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="it" resp="#VM">
                <p cert="low">[Dedicato] alle due dee.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" xml:lang="en" resp="#VM">
                <p>Only three letters are fully preserved; the traces after iota are most plausibly read as nu. 
                    The size of the writing field and of the letters suggests that the complete text comprised no more than approximately 
                    six or seven letters. The text is too fragmentary for a secure restoration and may preserve either an anthroponym or 
                    the name or epithet of a divinity. Mignosa in the ed. pr. consider ταῖν [θεαῖν], a dual dative formula referring to Demeter and Kore, 
                    the most plausible restoration. If a uniform letter module is assumed, this restoration would exceed the estimated available space by two letters. 
                    This is not necessarily decisive, however, since individual letters may vary in size even within the same inscription, as frequently occurs. 
                   The alternatives Ταιν[αρίᾳ], an epithet of Artemis, and Ταιν[άριος], an epithet of Poseidon, are regarded as less likely in the Selinuntine context. 
                    The archaeological setting, careful pre-firing execution and probable brevity of the text support its interpretation as a votive dedication, 
                    but the inscription does not securely identify the deity or deities worshipped in Temple R.</p>
            </div>
